# Build-context denylist for the apex (Level 1) JavaScript tier.
#
# The sibling Dockerfile copies its runtime files BY NAME, so this file does not
# decide what lands in the image -- it decides what the daemon has to receive
# before the build starts. Excluding .git alone is the difference between a
# context measured in megabytes and one measured in kilobytes.
#
# Keep this an explicit denylist. A catch-all "*" with "!" re-inclusions is
# harder to reason about, and one mis-ordered negation drops a file silently.
# An unanchored pattern matches at the context root only, which is enough here:
# every source file sits at the root and every excluded subtree is excluded as a
# whole directory.
#
# Never exclude package.json or config/health.json. health.js reads both at
# module load and falls back to compiled-in literals rather than failing, so
# their absence would leave the endpoint answering 200 with an identity this
# repository never declared -- a failure with no symptom. So no broad *.json,
# config or config/* pattern. ("*.test.js" below is safe: none of index.js,
# health.js or server.js ends in ".test.js".)

# Version-control metadata, its rules file and its submodule declaration. .git
# also carries local remote-tracking configuration that must not enter a layer.
.git
.gitignore
.gitmodules

# The child submodule's working tree. Each tier builds its own image from its
# own sources, so a Node image has no use for Python and Java trees. This is a
# build-context exclusion only; the submodule stays fully tracked in Git.
child_repo_10_LOC

# Build-time-only container definitions: read from the context before the build,
# useless inside a running container.
.dockerignore
Dockerfile

# A .github directory, whenever one is added. Anything under it is read from the
# repository rather than from inside an image, so it never belongs in a context.
.github

# Local environment files. A secrets file must never enter an image layer, and
# nothing here parses one anyway: this tier reads process.env directly. The
# pattern also covers the tracked .env.example, which is documentation.
.env*

# Installed dependency tree. This tier has none -- every module it loads is a
# Node built-in -- so this line exists to keep a stale host directory out of the
# context, not to protect the image: the Dockerfile copies named files only, so
# node_modules could not reach /app in any case.
node_modules

# Test sources. A runtime image ships no test.
*.test.js

# Documentation, including the normative contract document. health.js and
# server.js reference docs/health-endpoint.md in comments and never open it.
docs
README.md

# Execution residue from a local test or npm run. Not reproducible input.
coverage
.npm
npm-debug.log*
*.log

# Operating-system metadata that file managers leave behind. Never content.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Deliberately NOT excluded: .nvmrc. It pins the Node version for contributors
# and is unused at run time -- the image's Node comes from the base tag -- but it
# is eight bytes and keeps /app consistent with the repository. Python and Java
# residue patterns are absent because this tier cannot produce them; Levels 2
# and 3 exclude their own.
